EVERY CONDITIONAL-BRANCH AND SUPERVISOR-CALL HALFWORD.

Status: exhaustive 4096-halfword DECODING SUBTREE, with a deliberately
smaller English Major C execution permission grant. This is source-level
architecture work. Neither exhaustive ARMv7-A T32 decoding nor a working
English Major C compiler or interpreter has been accomplished.

The included range is exactly HEXADECIMAL 0xD000 through 0xDFFF, inclusive,
and NO other part of the T32 instruction-encoding space.

NORMATIVE ARCHITECTURE REFERENCE AND VERSION.

ARM Limited, ARM Architecture Reference Manual, ARMv7-A and ARMv7-R edition,
ARM DDI 0406C.d, publication ID040418, A6.2.6, Table A6-8, "Conditional
branch, and Supervisor Call". For specific instructions also consult
A8.8.18 B (the T1 encoding), A8 UDF and A8 SVC entries, and the
ARMv7-A application-level/exception model. The primary ARM source is
https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5f8daeb7f86e16515cdb8c4e
No different Arm profile or newer architecture is silently substituted.

The prior closed English Major C operation vocabulary is in the sibling
file "All and only these initial operations are permitted.english".
Operation 43 grants conditional branch requests in legal T32 forms.
The UDF and SVC machine encodings are NOT granted by that vocabulary;
their classification here does not confer an execution permission.

The entire 4096-row data-level enumeration is:
    _/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-conditional-branch-and-exception-halfwords.tsv
at the repository root. It records every numbered halfword, its classification,
condition or absence of one, encoded immediate, branch displacement when
applicable, initial permission and any relevant foreign-decoder alias.

PART ONE. FINITE AND EXHAUSTIVE CLASSIFICATION BY BITS.

Every halfword in this subtree has 16 bits numbered 15 down to zero.
The highest four bits, 15..12, are binary 1101.
The next four bits, 11..8, form an unsigned four-bit sub-opcode.
The lowest eight bits, 7..0, form the encoded immediate byte.

There are sixteen possible sub-opcodes and 256 possible immediate bytes.
Sixteen multiplied by 256 is 4096. Thus an enumeration that assigns
exactly one classification to each sub-opcode and every imm8, without
omissions or overlaps, covers every 16-bit encoding in this range.

For sub-opcodes 0000 through 1101 inclusive (hexadecimal 0 through D),
the instruction class is the SIXTEEN-BIT THUMB CONDITIONAL BRANCH T1.
Fourteen distinct sub-opcodes, each with 256 possible encoded immediate
bytes, give precisely 3584 conditional-branch encodings. They are all
instances of the existing long English operation 43, subject to its
instruction form, execution state, source-level target boundary and
conditional-flag semantics.

Sub-opcode 1110 (hexadecimal E), all 256 immediate values,
is UDF T1: permanently undefined instruction. This is NOT an
ordinary conditional branch, nor is it an invitation to use the
undefined encodings as implementation-defined arithmetic.

Sub-opcode 1111 (hexadecimal F), all 256 immediate values,
is SVC T1: Supervisor Call. This is an architectural request for
a supervisor exception and is NOT an ordinary conditional branch.
The same 8-bit field is NOT the signed branch displacement in this form.

These three classes partition the set with counts:
  3584 branch encodings;
   256 UDF encodings;
   256 SVC encodings;
  4096 total, no gaps and no overlap.

PART TWO. ALL FOURTEEN CONDITIONAL-BRANCH PREDICATES IN ENGLISH.

For this subtree only, the sub-opcode and the condition are:

  0 (EQ): take the branch when the zero flag Z is one.
  1 (NE): take the branch when the zero flag Z is zero.
  2 (CS/HS): take when the carry flag C is one.
  3 (CC/LO): take when the carry flag C is zero.
  4 (MI): take when negative flag N is one.
  5 (PL): take when negative flag N is zero.
  6 (VS): take when signed-overflow flag V is one.
  7 (VC): take when signed-overflow flag V is zero.
  8 (HI): take when C is one and Z is zero.
  9 (LS): take when C is zero or Z is one.
  A (GE): take when N and V are equal.
  B (LT): take when N and V differ.
  C (GT): take when Z is zero and N and V are equal.
  D (LE): take when Z is one or N and V differ.

The aliases "carry set" and "unsigned higher or same" refer to
the same flag test, as do "carry clear" and "unsigned lower".
They do not alter the encoded four-bit sub-opcode.

A signed comparison cannot generally be inferred merely from N;
V is essential in the presence of signed arithmetic overflow.
An unsigned comparison of two values cannot be substituted for
their signed comparison just because both contain 32 bits.
These conditions use the CURRENT flag values, whose origin is
a separate prior instruction and whose validity cannot be assumed.

The code 1110 is NOT an AL instruction here. It selects UDF.
The code 1111 is NOT a NEVER predicate here. It selects SVC.
The current profile must never claim fourteen branch conditions
plus two more hypothetical conditions for E and F.

PART THREE. EXACT COMPUTATION OF THE DISPLACEMENT.

Let the first halfword of the current instruction begin at BYTE ADDRESS A.
It is TWO bytes long.
Let the encoded immediate field imm8 be a number between zero and 255.
Interpret that field as an eight-bit two's-complement integer:
   if imm8 is less than 128, signed_imm8 = imm8;
   otherwise, signed_imm8 = imm8 minus 256.
Multiply this signed quantity by two:
   branch_displacement_in_bytes = 2 times signed_imm8.

This arithmetic is equivalent to sign-extending the nine-bit string
imm8 followed by a binary zero. Thus the displacement must be an
EVEN number in the closed interval -256 through +254, inclusive.

The architectural PC operand for this T32 T1 branch is A plus FOUR bytes.
If the branch condition is true, the new execution index is:
   (A plus four bytes) plus branch_displacement_in_bytes.
If the branch condition is false, the new execution index is:
   A plus TWO bytes.

The instruction itself does NOT overwrite R0..R12, SP, LR,
or N, Z, C and V. A successful taken branch must still enter an
architecturally valid executable T32 instruction boundary.
This closed English Major C profile requires a verified named
target entry and a target permission; a mathematical address alone
is not enough to authorize execution. A failed condition makes
no data-memory access and does not execute the target merely
because the target might have been prefetched.

The halfword with first hexadecimal byte 0xD0 and second byte 0xFF
is 0xD0FF. Its imm8 is 255, signed_imm8 is -1, and displacement is -2.
A taken branch starting at A selects A+2; a NOT-taken branch also
continues at A+2. The successor ADDRESSES coincide, but the
architectural predicate and the statement that the branch was
taken are still distinct facts.

A halfword 0xD0FE has immediate 254, representing signed -2,
hence displacement -4 and a taken target at A itself.
A halfword 0xD080 has immediate 128, representing -128,
hence displacement -256 and target A-252.
A halfword 0xD07F has immediate 127, hence displacement +254
and target A+258.

The halfword 0xD000 has encoded displacement ZERO. Its taken
target is A+4, not A+2. A source translation that says
"zero displacement means fall through" is therefore wrong.
If the instruction at A+2 is a four-byte instruction,
A+4 could name its second halfword. That numerical possibility
does NOT automatically make it a valid checked-source branch
entry. The target must be validated independently.

PART FOUR. T1 IS NOT THE SAME AS AN UNRESTRICTED IT BRANCH.

ARM DDI 0406C.d A8.8.18 expressly says:
the T1 conditional B form is NOT permitted inside an IT block;
in an IT block that encoding is architecturally UNPREDICTABLE.

This is materially different from the T2 unconditional B encoding,
which can be legal as the last instruction in an IT block, and from
other differently encoded branch forms. The words "conditional B"
alone do not encode a right to place a T1 B inside an IT block.

The initial grant's operation 43 permits multiple potential
T32 B encodings but DOES NOT promise that they share IT legality,
branch range or instruction length. The 3584 rows in the present
subtree are admitted to this grant ONLY OUTSIDE AN IT BLOCK.
A future compiler must select the correct concrete form after
checking ITSTATE and all legal conditions; otherwise it must reject.

Attempting to enter the middle of an IT block by branching to its
second or later instruction is also disallowed by the checked-source
execution model. That is not equivalent to jumping into an ordinary
linear instruction sequence with no surrounding ITSTATE.

PART FIVE. EXACT SEMANTIC BOUNDARY FOR UDF.

Every halfword from 0xDE00 through 0xDEFF decodes to the
architecture's permanently undefined T1 instruction class.
The low eight bits can be shown in an assembler UDF annotation,
but they do NOT create a conditional branch, set arithmetic
condition flags, or supply an eight-bit branch displacement.

Executing a UDF in an ordinary permitted Thumb environment
raises an Undefined Instruction exception, rather than
completing as a normal branch or advancing an English Major C
execution index by a successful ordinary step.
The operating system's response to the exception requires a
separate machine and process environment specification.

The English Major C source permission grant DOES NOT include
UDF, even when the underlying architecture defines the trapping
encoding. The decoder should identify the encountered bytes
as the actual UDF class and then report an UNGRANTED operation.
"Ungrantable" is not implied; a later language profile may
explicitly define intentional traps, but that is not done here.

A foreign LLVM 17 disassembler uses "__brkdiv0" for 0xDEF9
and "trap" for 0xDEFE. These are TOOL OUTPUT NAMES,
not additional ARM architectural instruction classes.
They were encountered during the independent full-range
decode check. Classifying them as ordinary successful
instructions would have been an error. Every DE00..DEFF
encoding belongs in the UDF class even when the disassembler
prints one of those aliases.

PART SIX. EXACT SEMANTIC BOUNDARY FOR SVC.

Every halfword from 0xDF00 through 0xDFFF is SVC T1
with an unsigned eight-bit immediate field. The encoded
byte does not become a signed PC-relative displacement.

SVC is a legitimate ARM architectural mechanism for a
program, including one executing in unprivileged user mode,
to request an exception into the operating system's
supervisor-handling mechanism. It is NOT generally "illegal
because user code cannot execute privileged instructions".
Its effect is an exception entry whose saved state and
return convention are specified in the architectural
exception rules, rather than an ordinary user instruction
that writes the next sequential execution index with no
other effects.

The immediate number is not universally the identity of an
operating-system service. The calling convention and the
interpretation of register contents and/or embedded immediate
depend on the actual OS/ABI. An ARM 32-bit Android/Linux user
process cannot safely infer a syscall number, kernel
entry point, or permission solely from 0xDFxx.

The initial English Major C operation profile does NOT grant
SVC. If a disassembled machine program contains DF00, the
English translator may identify its encoding but MUST NOT
translate it into a successful plain-English function call
or claim that its exception was executed or handled.
A separate exception/OS boundary needs to be admitted and
fully specified, including privilege, register effects,
saved state, and return or process termination.

PART SEVEN. THE REAL DIFFERENCE BETWEEN HARDWARE DECODING AND
SOFTWARE PERMISSION.

An architectural decoder can successfully recognize
a class of machine bytes without the current language
granting a program the right to express their effects.
This distinction is visible three different ways here:

First, conditional B is a well-defined instruction form,
and the current English Major C operation 43 allows
the correct long-name branch operation when the target,
flags, encoding and IT restrictions hold.

Second, UDF is a well-defined architecture-defined
instruction intended to generate an exception.
It is not permissible in this initial source profile
merely because the decoder knows its mnemonic.

Third, SVC is a well-defined mechanism to request
an exception from user code. It is also excluded
because its OS and exception transition has not
been admitted and described. It is NOT placed
in UDF's class to make rejection look simpler.

The 4096 rows of the associated TSV represent
DATA ABOUT MACHINE ENCODINGS. Reading or validating
a TSV row does not execute its encoded instruction.
Translating that data into a checked executable
operation is a separate legal/semantic step.

PART EIGHT. EVIDENCE OF A COMPLETE FINITE DECODE.

The associated TSV has 4096 unique rows, one for
each increasing numerical halfword in D000..DFFF.
The table columns distinguish:
  encoded halfword in hexadecimal;
  unsigned immediate byte;
  ARM instruction decode class;
  condition mnemonic or explicit absence;
  signed byte displacement or explicit absence;
  whether the initial long-name grant permits the form;
  diagnostic foreign-decoder alias when necessary.

Every row is derivable from the ARM Table A6-8 partition
and the displacement formula above. A validator must
calculate these facts independently from the halfword,
not merely trust that the TSV is the correct length.

A deliberately temporary diagnostic used clang 17.0.0,
target armv7a-none-eabi with Thumb state selected,
to assemble an ascending sequence of ALL 4096 .hword
values as data in a machine-code section. LLVM
objdump 17.0.0 then linearly disassembled those
halfwords using thumbv7-none-eabi decoding.

The independent diagnostic observed one decoding
per halfword and the expected 4096 original halfwords:
  fourteen branch-condition groups, 256 each;
  UDF 256, including two LLVM aliases;
  SVC 256.
It also checked all 3584 printed immediate
displacements against the signed-eight-bit,
scaled-by-two formula, with ZERO unexplained
encoding/classification/displacement differences.

Initial naive comparison FAILED for UDF because
LLVM printed "__brkdiv0" and "trap" for two values.
That is retained as an actual diagnostic finding,
not erased to manufacture a failure-free history.
After separately accounting for those two names
as disassembler aliases for the UDF encoding group,
the table and independent decoder agreed.
The ARM normative classification was not altered
to match the disassembler's informal spelling.

This diagnostic was not a maintained ICK or
Android NDK build, no instructions were run
on ARM hardware, and no first-party language
runtime executed a single step.

PART NINE. CHECKED EXAMPLES WITH NUMERIC ADDRESSES.

CASE ONE, equality branch, condition true:
  beginning instruction byte address A = 0x00001000;
  halfword = 0xD000; Z = 1.
  The branch displacement is zero.
  Its target is A+4 = 0x00001004.
  The next execution index is 0x00001004.
  Z is still 1.

CASE TWO, equality branch, condition false:
  the same A and halfword, but Z = 0.
  The condition fails; the instruction occupies two bytes.
  The next execution index is 0x00001002.
  The unchanged Z is 0.

CASE THREE, backwards equality branch:
  A = 0x00001010, halfword = 0xD0FD, Z=1.
  imm8=0xFD = unsigned 253 = signed -3.
  Displacement = -6 bytes. PC operand base = 0x1014.
  Target = 0x100E. No data memory access occurs.

CASE FOUR, self-loop when equal:
  A=0x00001010, halfword=0xD0FE, Z=1.
  Displacement=-4; PC operand base=0x1014.
  Target=0x1010, the same current instruction.
  A future execution may return to that instruction.
  This does not prove that an interrupt cannot occur.

CASE FIVE, signed versus unsigned:
  Compare first value -1 (bits FFFFFFFF)
  against second value 1 (bits 00000001).
  If flags correspond to the proper subtraction,
  an LT condition differs from a CC/LO condition.
  The bit pattern alone does not pick a signed
  comparison; the branch's condition code does.

CASE SIX, undefined-instruction boundary:
  halfword=0xDEFE. LLVM may display "trap";
  the architectural class is UDF T1.
  The initial grant has no English operation
  permitting the ensuing exception.
  A checked translation stops at the unsupported boundary.

CASE SEVEN, supervisor-call boundary:
  halfword=0xDF00. The encoded immediate is zero,
  but this is SVC, not a branch with zero displacement.
  A semantic trace without the OS exception model
  stops rather than guessing a particular syscall.

PART TEN. DELIBERATE DEFECTS THAT MUST BE REJECTED.

A future independent decoder and checker must reject:
  A. classifying any DE00..DEFF halfword as an EQ/NE branch;
  B. classifying any DF00..DFFF halfword as ordinary B;
  C. interpreting 1110 as AL or 1111 as a "never" condition;
  D. sign-extending an SVC immediate as a branch displacement;
  E. treating D0FF's -2 offset as an unsigned +510 offset;
  F. using A+2 as the PC-relative branch base in T32;
  G. assuming that a zero-encoded offset means A+2;
  H. advancing a two-byte T1 instruction as though it were
     a four-byte T32 instruction;
  I. changing N/Z/C/V merely by evaluating a conditional B;
  J. using a stale flag value after an intervening flag write;
  K. emitting a T1 conditional branch inside a Thumb IT block;
  L. confusing a taken B with a call that writes LR;
  M. claiming that SVC cannot be issued from unprivileged
     software solely because it enters a privileged handler;
  N. counting the diagnostic LLVM aliases "__brkdiv0"
     or "trap" as additional valid English operations;
  O. claiming that the whole ARM Thumb ISA is exhaustively
     described because a single 4096-encoding subtree is;
  P. conflating a raw halfword present as memory DATA with
     its successful decoding/execution as an instruction.

These are required acceptance criteria, NOT independent
first-party compiler or runtime passes at this revision.

PART ELEVEN. CONSIDERED EXECUTION MODELS, RETAINED.

Choice 1: use an explicit comparison plus short T1 conditional branch.
Advantage: a compact, direct two-byte branch with the condition code
in the instruction itself. Restriction: short signed displacement,
no entry into IT blocks, and a branch that may be data dependent.

Choice 2: use a wider T32 conditional B form.
Advantage: a greater branch reach may fit generated code.
Cost: four-byte instruction and a distinct encoding/IT contract.
The current instruction grant cannot treat those extra encodings
as identical simply because an assembler renders both as "beq".

Choice 3: choose an inverted condition and arrange the other
outcome as sequential fall-through.
Advantage: can affect the number of taken branches and instruction
layout for observed input distributions. Cost: code shape changes;
no performance claim follows without the actual CPU/predictor.

Choice 4: hoist an unchanging option test outside a loop, then
use a specialized branch arrangement. Advantage: avoid repeated
software work in the loop. Cost: larger code, one-time dispatch,
and correctness proof of the option's invariance.

Choice 5: use legal T32 IT predication for a short arithmetic
operation where the architecture allows it.
Advantage: may eliminate a separate control-transfer instruction.
Restriction: IT context and encoding limits; specifically T1 B
cannot be used in IT. Predicated arithmetic also has effects
and possible costs; it is not guaranteed faster.

Choice 6: form a numerical mask/selection and perform no data-dependent
branch for a particular computation. Advantage: one possible way
to avoid branch prediction variability. Cost: more instructions,
careful sign/mask/flag equivalence requirements and different
performance on different CPU implementations.

Choice 7: use the architectural SVC mechanism to request an
operating-system service as an explicitly separately typed
effect. Advantage: accesses the native OS boundary.
Restriction: privilege transition, exception saving and
OS-specific ABI; not allowed by the present profile.

Choice 8: permit deliberate undefined instructions as explicit
traps with a separately modeled exception result.
Advantage: makes a machine-level fault boundary expressible.
Restriction: no silent "skip this instruction" semantics.
This is NOT the current language's chosen treatment.

None of these choices has been selected as the universal
optimal branch scheme for R's sum(..., na.rm=TRUE).
The correct comparison remains tied to actual compiled
machine code and verified target hardware evidence.

END OF THE FINITE D000..DFFF DECODE CONTRACT.
